(Bug #87's siblings. The register found the declaration from a buffer
 variable, but a cast-free buffer lands in a `value` slot five other ways,
 and every one of them stored the buffer's struct pointer and printed the
 capacity byte - `@`, a 64-byte buffer's capacity low byte.

 The one code path is `generate_expr_as_text` (and through it bug #51's
 `emit_buffer_to_text_copy`), so this file is really asking whether every
 `value` write site reaches it, exactly as tests/386 asks it of the text
 write sites.)

To 'make a buffer'.
  a buffer called inner is 64 bytes in size.
  append "first" to inner.
  Return a buffer, inner.

To 'echo the value' with a value called what. Print what.
To 'hand back a value' with a buffer called source. Return a value, source.

a buffer called label is 64 bytes in size.
append "first" to label.

(a declaration from a buffer variable - the register's own repro)
a value called declared is label.
Print declared.

(a declaration from a buffer-returning call)
a value called relayed is 'make a buffer'.
Print relayed.

(`Set v to <buffer>`)
a value called assigned is "seed".
Set assigned to label.
Print assigned.

(`the v is <buffer>`)
a value called restated is "seed".
the restated is label.
Print restated.

(a `value` parameter receiving a buffer argument)
'echo the value' with label.

(`Return a value, <buffer>`)
a value called returned is 'hand back a value' with label.
Print returned.
